{
  "image_name": "2e9eae9e1580a19686314d7a783e1849.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure compares a healthy intestine with an inflamed intestine, highlighting how the mucosal barrier and underlying tissue change during inflammation. In the healthy state, the lumen contains a microbiome that is kept away from the epithelium by distinct outer and inner mucus layers. The epithelial lining includes specialized cells such as goblet, enteroendocrine, tuft, and stem cells arranged around crypt-like folds. Beneath this, the lamina propria contains scattered immune and stromal cells, including macrophages, monocytes, dendritic cells, T and B cells, plasma cells, and fibroblasts, with blood and lymphatic vessels running centrally.\n\nIn the inflamed intestine, the barrier appears disrupted. Bacteria are no longer confined to the lumen and seem to penetrate the mucus and approach the epithelial surface. The mucus layers look altered, and the tissue below shows a marked increase in immune cells, especially clustered lymphocytes and other inflammatory cells. Fibroblasts appear activated and more abundant, suggesting tissue remodeling. Overall, the image shows a transition from a well-organized, compartmentalized intestinal surface to a more permeable, immune-cell-rich environment associated with inflammation.",
